What:		/sys/kernel/debug/powerpc/nmmu_segtab
Date:		September 2026
Contact:	linuxppc-dev@lists.ozlabs.org
Description:	write only
		Write a process id, in decimal, to dump that process's nest
		MMU state to the kernel log: its hardware PID, its process
		table entry, and every valid entry of its segment table, each
		recomputed from the address space and marked "ok" or
		"MISMATCH". Present on 64-bit Book3S kernels built with
		CONFIG_DEBUG_FS; the tables exist only under the hashed page
		table, where the kernel builds them for the nest MMU
		(Documentation/arch/powerpc/vas.rst). Fails with ESRCH if
		there is no such process or it has no address space.

What:		/sys/kernel/debug/powerpc/nmmu_slices
Date:		September 2026
Contact:	linuxppc-dev@lists.ozlabs.org
Description:	write only
		Write a process id, in decimal, to dump that process's slice
		map to the kernel log: the address limit, the default page
		size, and the page size of each run of slices. This is the
		page size a segment table entry for the range must carry.
		Fails with ESRCH if there is no such process or it has no
		address space, and with ENODEV on a radix kernel.

What:		/sys/kernel/debug/powerpc/nmmu_stats
Date:		September 2026
Contact:	linuxppc-dev@lists.ozlabs.org
Description:	read only
		Counters and geometry of the nest MMU segment tables, one
		per line as "<name> <value>":

		ste_insert
			Segment table entries added.
		ste_present
			Insertions that found the entry already present.
		ste_nospc
			Insertions that found both entry groups full.
		ste_ovf_flush
			Tables discarded to make room for an entry.
		ste_nospc_fatal
			Insertions that found no room even after a flush.
		ste_efault
			Insertions for an address with no segment translation.
		segtab_flush
			Tables emptied by a page size change.
		hw_pid_alloc
			Hardware PIDs allocated.
		hw_pid_free
			Hardware PIDs retired.
		segtab_entries
			Entries one table holds.
		segtab_groups
			Entry groups one table holds.
		process_table
			"present" or "absent".
